  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 143 PM EST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nLight showers remain ongoing across portions of the area and\ncontinue overnight. The best chances for rain remain across the \nCumberland Plataea and southern TN Valley. Amounts less than one \ntenth are expected for areas that do see rain. Elsewhere, mostly \ndry conditions are in place outside of a few passing sprinkles, \ndue to the dry air at the surface. Downsloping winds across the \neast TN mountains are keeping areas east of I-75 mostly dry. \nAdditionally, mountaintop winds will remain breezy due to a \npersistent, southeast, 850mb jet of around 30kts. Our highest \nelevations, such as Cove Mountain, will continue to see gusts \nbetween 30 and 40 mph through Monday morning.\n\nTo start the week, high pressure is anchored in place across the \nCoastal Carolinas. Meanwhile, an upper trough will slowly advance \ntoward the region through Wednesday. This setup will allow for an \nincreased pressure gradient to remain in place across the region, \nand therefore an increased 850 mb jet of around 30 to 40 kts. This \nmeans mountaintop winds will continue to be breezy through \nmidweek. Cove Mountain may occasionally gust to 40 mph, or \nslightly above, but non-advisory gusts are mostly expected. High \npressure will also help to keep us dry until Wednesday's frontal \nboundary moves into the area. We can also expect above normal \ntemperatures due to rising heights with highs in the mid to upper\n70s, which is around 10 degrees above normal. This puts us only a\nfew degrees below our record highs for this time of year, which \nare in the lower 80s.\n\nChances for rain increase on Wednesday as a frontal boundary moves \nthrough the area. Chances for rain remain in place through Thursday \ndue to the slow moving boundary. During this time, storm-total \nrainfall amounts will generally range around 1 to 1.25 inches for \nthe Cumberland Plateau, central and southern TN Valley, and \nsouthwest NC. The eastern foothills, northeast TN, and southwest \nVA will see amounts closer to 0.5 to 1 inch. \n\nWe begin to dry out on Friday behind the front with dry \nconditions continuing into the weekend. This also means cooler \ntemperatures, with current forecast highs over the weekend in low \nto mid 60s.",
